---
import Cards from './Cards.astro';
/* The pinned fly-apart headline.

   Cloned from kora.framer.media (Framer's "Inside Container" > "Text /
   Comparison Cards"). The full extraction — the 24-position scrub, the
   breakpoint table, the type ramp — is in
   docs/research/components/kora-changes.spec.md.

   The headline is TWO BLOCKS, not split text, because that is what the original
   is: the break falls after "when", and the two halves leave in opposite
   directions. Splitting a single line per-word would have made the mechanism
   harder, not easier — there is no per-glyph stagger here to earn it back.

   It is a departure, not an arrival. The line rests centred and readable, and
   scrolling pushes it away from the camera and off both edges. Getting that
   backwards is the one thing that would make this read as a different section.

   The <span> break is inside the first half on purpose: "when" is already grey
   while it is still on line one, so the colour change and the line break happen
   at different words. That mismatch is the detail that makes the two-tone read
   as deliberate rather than as a line-by-line fade. */
/* The claim, then the evidence.

   THE ASTERISK IS THE PIVOT, and that is not decoration. The loader already sets
   a mark beside its word (.ld-ast), and section two's curved sentence turns on a
   '*' between its two clauses. Using it a third time here, at the exact point
   where the two halves fly apart, makes it a system instead of a coincidence —
   the mark means "and here is the turn" every time it appears on this site.

   The sentence is built for the split: the count goes left, the qualifier goes
   right, and the mark sits on the seam between them. It also earns its keep as
   copy — four cards follow, and "one pair of hands" is the claim section two's
   sentence already makes ("I do both, in order") without ever boasting.

   Colour is --ink, the loader's black, for the whole line. Kora's two-tone grey
   was carrying its contrast; here the motion does that, so the type can stay
   undivided and let the one mark do the work. */
const A = 'Four teams';
const B = 'one pair of hands.';
---
<section class="chg" data-changes>
  <div class="chg-pin">
    <div class="chg-text">
      <div class="chg-half" data-chg-half="l">
        <h2 class="chg-h">{A}<span class="chg-ast" aria-hidden="true">*</span></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="chg-half" data-chg-half="r">
        <h2 class="chg-h">{B}</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Cards />
  </div>
</section>
